%!
% The interpreter copies a wrapped operator's operands aside before its
% procedure runs, into an array it keeps in privatedict under .wrapsave.
% That array is machinery, but privatedict is reachable -- a program
% stores under any key there with a plain put -- so what stands under
% .wrapsave is the program's to replace.
%
% The array is dereferenced against local memory and its declared size
% is the bound the copy is held to. Both hold only while the array is
% local. A global array stored there is read at the local entity of the
% same number -- a different object, of a different size -- and the copy,
% bounded by the global array's declared size rather than that object's,
% is written past its end into whatever local memory lies beyond.
%
% A program-allocated global array takes a high entity number, so the
% collision needs the local table grown past it first. This grows it
% with small arrays of a known marker, installs a large global array
% where the copy writes, runs one wrapped operator with operands live,
% and then reads the markers back: a copy that stayed within the array
% it was given leaves every one of them alone.

% a large global array: large so the copy's bound check, which reads the
% array's declared size, admits the write
currentglobal true setglobal
/g 500 array def
setglobal
g 0 1 put

% grow the local table past the global array's entity number with small
% arrays whose first element is the small integer the copy's guard reads
% as a starting offset
/N 20000 def
/fillers N array def
0 1 N 1 sub { /i exch def fillers i [ 1 1 1 1 ] put } for

% distinctive operands, then the global array installed where the copy
% writes, then one wrapped operator to run the copy
/S 88801 def
S S 1 add S 2 add S 3 add S 4 add S 5 add S 6 add S 7 add
.privatedict /.wrapsave g put
==
clear

% a copy the wrapped operator above ran finds the global array under
% .wrapsave and, being asked for a local one, rebuilds a local array in
% its place, so the wrapped operators the scan below runs are sound

% the markers: none is touched by a copy held to the array it was given
/bad 0 def
0 1 N 1 sub {
  /i exch def
  fillers i get { 1 ne { /bad bad 1 add def } if } forall
} for

bad 0 eq
  { (SUCCESS) = }
  { (FAILURE: a global .wrapsave array wrote past a local object; )
    print bad 32 string cvs print ( markers overwritten) = }
ifelse
quit
